\begin{proof}
Let $G$ be a group of order $9$. If $G$ has an element of order $9$, then $G$ is cyclic and therefore abelian.

Otherwise, every nonidentity element has order $3$. Choose $a\neq e$ and set $A=\langle a\rangle$, so $|A|=3$. Since $[G:A]=3$, the subgroup $A$ has index $3$. The conjugation action of $G$ on the left cosets of $A$ gives a homomorphism $G\to S_3$. Its image has order dividing both $9$ and $6$, hence has order $1$ or $3$. In particular, the kernel has order at least $3$.

Alternatively, applying the class equation directly, every noncentral conjugacy class has size divisible by $3$, so $|Z(G)|$ is divisible by $3$. If $|Z(G)|=9$, then $G$ is abelian. If $|Z(G)|=3$, then $|G/Z(G)|=3$, so $G/Z(G)$ is cyclic. A group with cyclic quotient by its center is abelian: writing each element as $g^iz$ with $z\in Z(G)$ shows that all elements commute. Thus $G$ is abelian in every case.
\end{proof}
